          "case_name": "S.L. v. A.L.",
          "citation": "735 A.2d 433 (Del. Fam. Ct. 1999)",
          "court": "Delaware Family Court",
          "year": 1999,
          "date_decided": "1999-03-12",
          "holding": "The Family Court dismissed a minor's emancipation petition for want of subject-matter jurisdiction, holding that the Family Court is a court of limited statutory jurisdiction and cannot entertain a proceeding for which the Delaware Code supplies no statutory basis.",
          "relevance": "The closest Delaware authority on a youth's own petition to alter custodial status as majority approaches, and the jurisdictional predicate such a petition requires.",
          "quote": "Therefore, in order for this Court to find jurisdiction over a Petition for Emancipation, the Delaware Code must provide the Court with a statutory basis for the proceeding.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5136945/sl-v-al/",
